<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The global economy is under threat from th=
e energy price shock, record public debt and the AI investment boom, accord=
ing to the International Monetary Fund=E2=80=99s managing director, Kristal=
ina Georgieva.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In a speech before the IMF and World Bank =
annual meetings in Bangkok next week, she said the world is being pulled in=
 two directions =E2=80=93 a negative energy supply shock from the Middle Ea=
st war and a positive demand shock from artificial intelligence that is als=
o driving inflation higher.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CThe combined impact of these two =
forces is highly uneven across the world,=E2=80=9D she said, noting that th=
e AI boom is bypassing many countries.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Growing government debt is another major w=
orry. Georgieva singled out advanced economies, led by the US, as the =E2=
=80=9Cworst offenders=E2=80=9D on debt burdens, with debt to GDP ratios hig=
her than emerging markets and low-income countries.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Asian shares are down, while on Wall Stree=
t, the S&amp;P 500 and the Nasdaq finished at new all-time highs. The S&amp=
;P 500 rose almost 0.6% to 7,818.93, while the Nasdaq closed at 27,599.886.=
</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">MSCI=E2=80=99s broadest index of Asia-Paci=
fic shares excluding Japan fell 0.3%. Japan=E2=80=99s Nikkei lost 0.6%, Hon=
g Kong=E2=80=99s Hang Seng fell 0.5%, the Singapore market was down 1.3% an=
d South Korea=E2=80=99s Kospi tumbled nearly 2%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Oil prices have risen back above $100 a ba=
rrel again. Brent crude is up 0.66% at $101.19 a barrel, while US crude is =
0.5% ahead at $89.86 a barrel.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The UK housing market has come to a stands=
till, ahead of the introduction of the government=E2=80=99s Your First Home=
 scheme targeted at first-time buyers.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The latest figures from Lloyds Banking Gro=
up show prices were flat last month, after a 0.3% dip in August. The averag=
e property now costs =C2=A3298,441, while annual growth was also flat.</p>
